(5731530, 832383, 70369, '<td valign="top">Holy cow.. .I''ve never seen comments like this and they bum me out for you...actually, I''m shocked..  I''m sorry and I hope you don''t let it get you down.. I''m not sure why it''s not ok to do what you did, but it is ok for this public lashing.. one reason this site really sucks sometimes is the nasty words left for others.... No matter to me as I don''t care about following the "rules" in this context... Your image didn''t break any rule THIS SITE has implemented..It''s a nice shot and it deserved the spot the voters gave it..</td>', 'Holy cow.. .I''ve never seen comments like this and they bum me out for you...actually, I''m shocked..  I''m sorry and I hope you don''t let it get you down.. I''m not sure why it''s not ok to do what you did, but it is ok for this public lashing.. one reason this site really sucks sometimes is the nasty words left for others.... No matter to me as I don''t care about following the "rules" in this context... Your image didn''t break any rule THIS SITE has implemented..It''s a nice shot and it deserved the spot the voters gave it..', '2009-11-18 01:12:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(5731659, 832383, 100393, '<td valign="top">My word, I have to say I am a little taken aback by the hostility here - at least you were spared a ribbon, you''d have been crucified (photo opoportunity for ''religion''?). We all knew that there was potential for people to actually shoot wherever they wanted. There are a whole bunch of images that could''ve been taken anywhere; what mattered in this challenge was whether the photographer made an interesting image from what was in front of them - I think you did an OK job with a great scene, nothing amazing or unique but competent.<br/> <br/>Come on people no one died and I read the notes as written with a clear and cheeky sense of humour, nothing malicious at all (can that be said for all of the comments?). You really think misjudging an audience and deploying a poorly received bit of jokery warrants this degree of abuse?<br/> <br/>We see DNMC all of the time. Is this really so different?<br/> <br/>For me, the most important thing is that we all had a legitimate opportunity to ask ourselves if Nuzzer made the most of this scene - did you take yours? Or were you seduced by the scenery?<br/> <br/>If people continue to score on the quality of the eye candy rather than scoring photographic interpretation and vision, then this is an inevitable consequence... and perhaps even a useful reminder.<br/> <br/>[edited for punctuation - initially composed on my iPhone]<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2009-11-18 08:26:00.</i></td>', 'My word, I have to say I am a little taken aback by the hostility here - at least you were spared a ribbon, you''d have been crucified (photo opoportunity for ''religion''?). We all knew that there was potential for people to actually shoot wherever they wanted. There are a whole bunch of images that could''ve been taken anywhere; what mattered in this challenge was whether the photographer made an interesting image from what was in front of them - I think you did an OK job with a great scene, nothing amazing or unique but competent. Come on people no one died and I read the notes as written with a clear and cheeky sense of humour, nothing malicious at all (can that be said for all of the comments?). You really think misjudging an audience and deploying a poorly received bit of jokery warrants this degree of abuse? We see DNMC all of the time. Is this really so different? For me, the most important thing is that we all had a legitimate opportunity to ask ourselves if Nuzzer made the most of this scene - did you take yours? Or were you seduced by the scenery? If people continue to score on the quality of the eye candy rather than scoring photographic interpretation and vision, then this is an inevitable consequence... and perhaps even a useful reminder. [edited for punctuation - initially composed on my iPhone]', '2009-11-18 02:29:59.000000', '2009-11-18 08:26:00.000000', 0),
